---
import DocLayout from '../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
---

<DocLayout title="About" description="What Keld is and the design philosophy behind it">
  <h1>About Keld</h1>
  <p>
    Keld is a personal design system for brand, websites, and web apps. It is token-first
    and framework-agnostic — the entire system ships as plain CSS custom properties
    with no build step, no runtime dependencies, and no framework assumptions.
  </p>

  <h2>Aesthetic Direction</h2>
  <p>
    Clean, minimal, humanist — sharp, warm, intellectual. Familiar but differentiated.
    Keld aims for the quality of feeling considered rather than generic: warm enough to have
    character, restrained enough to not distract from content.
  </p>
  <p>
    The name and palette draw from northern European forest landscapes — birch, fern, iron, ash.
    These aren't decorative references; they shaped the specific colour choices: the slightly
    desaturated warmth of Birch Storm, the cool depth of Forest, the weight of Iron Night.
  </p>

  <h2>Four Palettes</h2>
  <div class="palette-grid">
    <div class="palette-card">
      <div class="palette-swatches">
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#0D1117"></span>
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#374151"></span>
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#4B5563"></span>
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#6B7280"></span>
      </div>
      <p class="palette-name">Iron Night</p>
      <p class="palette-desc">Dark surfaces — backgrounds, borders, selections.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="palette-card">
      <div class="palette-swatches">
        <span class="swatch swatch-light" style="background:#E4E2DA"></span>
        <span class="swatch swatch-light" style="background:#EEEDE9"></span>
        <span class="swatch swatch-light" style="background:#F5F4F0"></span>
      </div>
      <p class="palette-name">Birch Storm</p>
      <p class="palette-desc">Light surfaces — backgrounds, hover states.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="palette-card">
      <div class="palette-swatches">
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#5C9E86"></span>
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#3E7A62"></span>
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#2D5E4A"></span>
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#254D5A"></span>
      </div>
      <p class="palette-name">Forest</p>
      <p class="palette-desc">Accent — primary actions, CTAs, links, focus rings.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="palette-card">
      <div class="palette-swatches">
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#C5414C"></span>
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#AB5A2B"></span>
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#8D6B20"></span>
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#567A37"></span>
        <span class="swatch" style="background:#885DB4"></span>
      </div>
      <p class="palette-name">Bloom</p>
      <p class="palette-desc">Semantic states — error, annotation, warning, success, special.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two-Layer Architecture</h2>
  <p>
    Palette tokens are never used directly in components. A semantic layer maps
    purpose-named tokens to the correct palette value per theme mode:
  </p>
  <pre><code>/* Layer 1 — palette (defined once, never changes) */
--fr1-fern: #3E7A62;

/* Layer 2 — semantic (resolves differently per mode) */
[data-theme="light"] &#123; --color-accent: var(--fr1-fern); &#125;
[data-theme="dark"]  &#123; --color-accent: var(--fr0-sage); &#125;</code></pre>
  <p>
    Components reference <code>--color-accent</code> — never <code>--fr1-fern</code> directly.
    When the mode switches, all components update automatically with no conditional logic.
  </p>

  <h2>Design Principles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Proximity signals relationship; distance signals importance.</strong> The spacing scale encodes this — small gaps within components, large gaps between sections.</li>
    <li><strong>Motion is functional, not decorative.</strong> Every transition communicates state change. When in doubt, don't animate.</li>
    <li><strong>Accessibility is built in, not bolted on.</strong> Every colour pair is tested for WCAG contrast. Focus rings are never removed. Touch targets are always at least 44&times;44px.</li>
    <li><strong>Semantic tokens over raw palette.</strong> Components never hardcode colours. The token layer handles light, dark, and surface overrides automatically.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Keld Is Not</h2>
  <p>
    Keld is not a component library. It does not ship pre-built buttons, cards, or layouts
    as importable components. It ships the token foundations and specifications that make
    building components consistent and correct. The CSS spec is the deliverable;
    implementations live in each project.
  </p>
  <p>No Tailwind. No CSS-in-JS. No runtime. Just custom properties.</p>
</DocLayout>

<style>
  .palette-grid &#123;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-md);
    margin: var(--space-md) 0 var(--space-lg);
  &#125;

  .palette-card &#123;
    background: var(--color-bg-elevated);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: var(--space-md);
  &#125;

  .palette-swatches &#123;
    display: flex;
    gap: 4px;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  &#125;

  .swatch &#123;
    display: block;
    width: 28px;
    height: 28px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  &#125;

  .swatch-light &#123; box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(0,0,0,0.08); &#125;

  .palette-name &#123;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin-bottom: 2px;
  &#125;

  .palette-desc &#123;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: 0;
  &#125;
</style>
